IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wills-dates`

-- Wills Act 1968 (ACT) -- the provisions a person claiming under a will, or about one, meets: whether the will was
-- validly made, altered, revoked or revived; whether a court can save or correct it, and by when to apply; what a
-- later marriage or a divorce does to it; and whether a gift lapses or passes to the beneficiary's children.
--
-- SEAT: the person claiming under or about a will -- a beneficiary or would-be beneficiary, a member of the will-maker's
-- family, the executor named in it, and the person who applies to the Supreme Court (for rectification, for a court
-- authorised will for someone without testamentary capacity, or a child asking to make or revoke a will). "Is the will
-- valid?", "Does the alteration count?", "By when must I apply for rectification?", "Did the marriage revoke the will?",
-- "Does the gift to the former spouse survive the divorce?", "Does the gift lapse?", "What is my share?". The Supreme
-- Court and the registrar get no seat: what the court must be satisfied of is an input ("the Supreme Court is
-- satisfied", "has not yet decided") and the answer says what follows either way.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, a date, a number, a choice from a list). No question takes a DEONTIC or an
-- EVENT. The one duty with a deadline -- to apply for rectification within 6 months (s 12A (3)) -- is a regulative rule
-- run by EVALTRACE inside the question.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 8, s 8A, s 8B, s 9, s 10, s 11A, s 12, s 12A, s 12B, s 15C, s 15D, s 15H, s 16, s 16A-16F, s 16I,
-- s 16K (schedule 1 as a fact), s 20, s 20A, s 21, s 22, s 31, s 31B, s 31C, s 35. Folded into the answers' words:
-- ss 13-15, 18, 19 (witnesses), s 10 (3), s 20A (3), s 31C (4), (5). Out (NOTHING here; reasons in NOTES.md): s 5, s 7,
-- s 11, s 14A, ss 15A, 15B, 15E-15G, 16G, 16H, 16J, 16L, 16M, ss 17, 23-30A, 31A, 32, 33 and the schedule's articles.
--
-- Edition: Wills Act 1968 (ACT), A1968-11, Republication No 22 (R22), effective 23 February 2026, deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/A1968-11.txt. No regulation sets a number this encoding needs.
--
-- (c) Australian Capital Territory. Used under CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md). Unofficial text.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Act hands to the Supreme Court are INPUTS, and the
-- answers say what follows from each. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md).

§ `Wills Act 1968 (ACT): the questions of a person claiming under or about a will`

-- What the Supreme Court has decided, for every provision that turns on the court being satisfied.
DECLARE `Supreme Court's view` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court has not yet decided`
    `the Supreme Court is satisfied`
    `the Supreme Court is not satisfied`

§§ `Which version of the Act applies (s 35, s 15H)`

-- s 35 (2): the provisions in the table's column 3, "as amended or inserted by the Wills (Amendment) Act 1991 ... apply
-- only in relation to wills taking effect after 7 November 1991" (ss 9, 11, 11A, 12, 12A, 12B, 14A, 15, 20, 20A, 30A, 31,
-- 31B, 31C). A will takes effect on the will-maker's death (s 24), so the date asked is the date of death (fork F4).
-- "only": the text's own no for a death on or before that day. The other questions assume a later death.
@export Do the 1991 provisions of the Wills Act 1968 (ACT) -- formal requirements (s 9), the court's power to accept an informal document (s 11A), alterations (s 12), rectification (s 12A), evidence (s 12B), divorce (s 20A), gifts to issue and the 30-day survival rule (ss 31, 31B, 31C) -- apply to this will (s 35 (2))? Only to a will taking effect after 7 November 1991, that is, where the will-maker died after that day.
GIVEN `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc The day the will-maker died (ISO date, e.g. 2025-03-15)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 35 -- do the 1991 provisions apply to the will` `the date of death` IF
    (`the calendar date` 7 11 1991) `falls before` `the date of death`

§§ `A will made by a child (s 8, s 8A, s 8B)`

-- s 8 (1): "Subject to this section and section 16, a will made by a child is not valid." (2) a child "who is or has been
-- married or in a civil union may make a valid will"; (3) a will made "in contemplation of a marriage or civil union is, on
-- the solemnisation of the marriage or entry into the civil union contemplated, valid"; (4) a will in the terms of a
-- s 8A order is valid; s 16 (7): age does not exclude a person from the s 16 classes. "child" is not defined in this
-- Act; the Legislation Act 2001 (not deposited) defines it as an individual under 18, asked as the age (fork F5).
-- s 8 (7): the section "has effect subject to section 9" -- the will still has to be made as s 9 requires.
DECLARE `What s 8 says about a child's will` IS ONE OF
    `s 8 is no bar: the will-maker was not a child when the will was made`
    `valid as to age: the child was or had been married or in a civil union (s 8 (2))`
    `valid as to age: made in the terms of a Supreme Court order under s 8A (s 8 (4))`
    `valid as to age: made in contemplation of a marriage or civil union that has since taken place (s 8 (3))`
    `not yet valid: made in contemplation of a marriage or civil union that has not taken place (s 8 (3))`
    `valid as to age: a privileged will under s 16, which a child may make (s 8 (1), s 16 (7))`
    `not valid: a will made by a child (s 8 (1))`

@export Does the will-maker's age stop the will being valid (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 8)? A will made by a child (under 18) is not valid unless the child was or had been married or in a civil union, made it in the terms of a Supreme Court order, made it in contemplation of a marriage or civil union that then took place, or made it as a member of the Defence Force on active service (s 16). The will must still be signed and witnessed (s 9).
GIVEN `age when made` IS A NUMBER @desc The will-maker's age in whole years on the day the will was made
      `married when made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when the will was made, the will-maker was or had been married or in a civil union
      `s 8A order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in the specific terms of a Supreme Court order under s 8A enabling the child to make it
      `in contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made in contemplation of a particular marriage or civil union
      `that marriage took place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that contemplated marriage or civil union has since taken place
      `privileged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker was, when making it, in one of the s 16 classes (Defence Force member in actual armed service, a representative abroad of an organisation serving the Defence Force, or a prisoner of war or internee from those classes)
GIVETH A `What s 8 says about a child's will`
`s 8 -- does my age stop the will being valid` `age when made` `married when made` `s 8A order` `in contemplation` `that marriage took place` `privileged` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `age when made` AT LEAST 18
            THEN `s 8 is no bar: the will-maker was not a child when the will was made`
        IF `married when made`
            THEN `valid as to age: the child was or had been married or in a civil union (s 8 (2))`
        IF `s 8A order`
            THEN `valid as to age: made in the terms of a Supreme Court order under s 8A (s 8 (4))`
        IF `in contemplation` AND `that marriage took place`
            THEN `valid as to age: made in contemplation of a marriage or civil union that has since taken place (s 8 (3))`
        IF `privileged`
            THEN `valid as to age: a privileged will under s 16, which a child may make (s 8 (1), s 16 (7))`
        IF `in contemplation`
            THEN `not yet valid: made in contemplation of a marriage or civil union that has not taken place (s 8 (3))`
        OTHERWISE `not valid: a will made by a child (s 8 (1))`

-- s 8A (1) "A child may apply to the Supreme Court for an order declaring that the child is entitled to make a will in
-- the terms of a proposed will"; s 8B (1) "A child who has made a valid will and has not at any time been married or in
-- a civil union may apply ... [to] revoke the will"; (2) of each: the court "may, if it is satisfied" that the child
-- understands the proposed will, it reflects the child's intentions, and it is reasonable -- "make an order". s 8 (2):
-- a married child needs no order.
DECLARE `What I want to do` IS ONE OF
    `make a will`
    `revoke my will or part of it`

DECLARE `Can I ask the court about my will as a child` IS ONE OF
    `you are not a child: you need no order`
    `you need no order: a child who is or has been married or in a civil union may make or revoke a will (s 8 (2))`
    `s 8B is not open to you: you have no valid will to revoke`
    `you may apply; the Supreme Court may make the order only if satisfied that you understand it, it reflects your intentions and it is reasonable (s 8A (2), s 8B (2))`
    `the Supreme Court may make the order: it is satisfied of the s 8A (2) or s 8B (2) matters`
    `the Supreme Court cannot make the order: it is not satisfied of the s 8A (2) or s 8B (2) matters`

@export As a child (under 18), can I ask the Supreme Court to let me make a will, or revoke the one I made (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 8A, s 8B)? You may apply with a proposed will (or revoking instrument) attached; the court may make the order if satisfied you understand its nature and effect, it accurately reflects your intentions and it is reasonable in all the circumstances.
GIVEN `my age` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in whole years
      `ever married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are or have been married or in a civil union
      `what I want` IS A `What I want to do` @desc Whether you want to make a will, or revoke a will (or part of one) you made
      `I have a valid will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For revoking: whether you have made a valid will (for example under a s 8A order)
      `the court's view` IS A `Supreme Court's view` @desc Whether the Supreme Court, on your application, is satisfied that you understand the proposed will or instrument, it accurately reflects your intentions, and it is reasonable that you make it
GIVETH A `Can I ask the court about my will as a child`
`s 8A, s 8B -- can I ask the court to let me make or revoke my will` `my age` `ever married` `what I want` `I have a valid will` `the court's view` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `my age` AT LEAST 18
            THEN `you are not a child: you need no order`
        IF `ever married`
            THEN `you need no order: a child who is or has been married or in a civil union may make or revoke a will (s 8 (2))`
        IF `what I want` EQUALS `revoke my will or part of it` AND (NOT `I have a valid will`)
            THEN `s 8B is not open to you: you have no valid will to revoke`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is satisfied`
            THEN `the Supreme Court may make the order: it is satisfied of the s 8A (2) or s 8B (2) matters`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is not satisfied`
            THEN `the Supreme Court cannot make the order: it is not satisfied of the s 8A (2) or s 8B (2) matters`
        OTHERWISE `you may apply; the Supreme Court may make the order only if satisfied that you understand it, it reflects your intentions and it is reasonable (s 8A (2), s 8B (2))`

§§ `Signing and witnessing (s 9, s 10)`

-- s 9 (1): "a will is not valid unless -- (a) it is in writing; and (b) it is signed at the foot or end by the testator, or
-- by another person in the presence of and by the direction of the testator; and (c) the signature ... is made or
-- acknowledged ... by the testator in the presence of 2 or more witnesses present at the same time; and (d) 2 or more of
-- those witnesses each attest ... and subscribe the will in the presence of the testator and of the other witness or
-- witnesses." s 10 (1): a signature placed elsewhere is not invalid as to its position if "it is apparent on the face of
-- the will that the testator intended to give effect by that signature to the writing signed as his or her will".
-- s 9 (2): no form of attestation clause is needed. ss 13-15, 18, 19: a witness who did not know it was a will, who is
-- or becomes incompetent, who (or whose domestic partner) benefits under it, a creditor or an executor does not make the
-- will invalid -- so none of these is asked.
DECLARE `Who signed` IS ONE OF
    `the will-maker`
    `another person, in the will-maker's presence and by the will-maker's direction`
    `another person, but not in the will-maker's presence or not by the will-maker's direction`
    `nobody`

DECLARE `Where the signature is` IS ONE OF
    `at the foot or end`
    `elsewhere, but placed so that it is apparent on the face of the document that it was meant to give effect to it (s 10)`
    `elsewhere, and it is not apparent that it was meant to give effect to the document`

GIVEN `the signer` IS A `Who signed`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the signer` `signs for the will-maker` IF
        `the signer` EQUALS `the will-maker`
    OR  `the signer` EQUALS `another person, in the will-maker's presence and by the will-maker's direction`

GIVEN `the place` IS A `Where the signature is`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the place` `is a good place for the signature` IF
    (NOT (`the place` EQUALS `elsewhere, and it is not apparent that it was meant to give effect to the document`))

@export Was the will signed and witnessed as the Wills Act 1968 (ACT) requires (s 9, s 10)? It must be in writing; signed by the will-maker (or by someone else in their presence and at their direction) at the foot or end, or anywhere it is apparent the signature was meant to give effect to the will; the signature made or acknowledged before 2 or more witnesses present together; and 2 or more of those witnesses must sign in the will-maker's presence and each other's. A witness who benefits under the will does not make it invalid (s 15). Anything written below the signature or added after it is not given effect (s 10 (3)).
GIVEN `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is in writing (handwritten, typed or printed)
      `the signer` IS A `Who signed` @desc Who signed the will
      `the place` IS A `Where the signature is` @desc Where on the will the signature is
      `witnesses present` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses were present together when the will-maker signed, or acknowledged the signature
      `witnesses who signed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those witnesses signed the will in the will-maker's presence and in the presence of the other witness or witnesses
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 9 -- was the will signed and witnessed as required` `in writing` `the signer` `the place` `witnesses present` `witnesses who signed` IF
        `in writing`
    AND `the signer` `signs for the will-maker`
    AND `the place` `is a good place for the signature`
    AND `witnesses present` AT LEAST 2
    AND `witnesses who signed` AT LEAST 2
    AND `witnesses who signed` AT MOST `witnesses present`

§§ `Is the will valid as to form (s 9, s 11A, Part 2A, s 16, s 16F, s 16I, Part 3B)`

-- s 16 (6): the classes of persons who may make a privileged will (oral or written declaration), s 16 (1).
DECLARE `Defence Force class` IS ONE OF
    `a member of the Defence Force in actual armed service`
    `a person employed outside Australia representing an organisation giving philanthropic, welfare or medical service to Defence Force members`
    `a prisoner of war or internee who, immediately before capture or internment, was in one of those two classes`
    `none of these`

-- s 15C and s 15D (1) (a), (b): the laws under which a will "shall be taken to have been properly made". s 15H: Part 2A
-- applies only to a will-maker who dies after its commencement (6 October 1983, Ord1983-46, from the endnotes). A
-- revoking will (s 15D (1) (c)) and powers of appointment (s 15D (2)) are out.
DECLARE `Law the will was made under` IS ONE OF
    `only the formal rules of the ACT Wills Act`
    `the law of the place where the will was made`
    `the law of the place where the will-maker was domiciled when making it or at death`
    `the law of the place where the will-maker habitually lived when making it or at death`
    `the law of the country of which the will-maker was a national or citizen when making it or at death`
    `the law of the place most closely connected with the ship or aircraft it was made on`
    `the law of the place where the land it deals with is (for that land only)`

DECLARE `Is the will valid as to form` IS ONE OF
    `valid: signed and witnessed as s 9 requires`
    `valid: a privileged will of a person on active service or in a like class (s 16)`
    `valid: an international will under the convention (s 16K, schedule 1)`
    `valid: a will made under a Supreme Court order, in writing, signed by the registrar and sealed (s 16F)`
    `valid: a statutory will made under the law of the place where the person lived (s 16I)`
    `valid: properly made under the law of a place connected with the will-maker (Part 2A, s 15C, s 15D)`
    `valid so far as it deals with land in the place whose law it followed (s 15D (1) (b))`
    `a will: the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended the document to be their will (s 11A)`
    `not a will: the Supreme Court is not satisfied the deceased intended it to be their will (s 11A)`
    `not valid as made; it is a will only if the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended it to be (s 11A)`
    `not valid as made, and s 11A is only for a document of a person who has died`

@export Is this will valid as to its form under the Wills Act 1968 (ACT)? It is if it was signed and witnessed as s 9 requires; or made by a member of the Defence Force on active service or a person in a like class (s 16); or made as an international will; or made under a Supreme Court order for a person without capacity (s 16F) or as a statutory will under the law where the person lived (s 16I); or made under the law of a place connected with the will-maker (Part 2A). If none applies, a deceased person's document is still a will if the Supreme Court is satisfied they intended it to be (s 11A).
GIVEN `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document is in writing
      `the signer` IS A `Who signed` @desc Who signed the document
      `the place` IS A `Where the signature is` @desc Where on the document the signature is
      `witnesses present` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses were present together when the will-maker signed, or acknowledged the signature
      `witnesses who signed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those witnesses signed in the will-maker's presence and in the presence of the other witness or witnesses
      `class` IS A `Defence Force class` @desc Whether the will-maker, when making it (orally or in writing), was in one of the s 16 classes
      `international will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made as an international will, following the convention's annex (declared before 2 witnesses and an authorised person such as an Australian legal practitioner or notary, signed and attested before them)
      `court-ordered will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will was made or altered under a Supreme Court order for a person without testamentary capacity, is in writing, and was signed by the registrar and sealed while the person was alive
      `statutory will elsewhere` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a statutory will (made for a person without testamentary capacity) under the law of the place where the person lived when it was executed
      `other law` IS A `Law the will was made under` @desc If the will followed the formal rules of another place's law, which connection that place has
      `the will-maker has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker has died
      `the date of death` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day of death (any date otherwise)
      `the court's view` IS A `Supreme Court's view` @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended the document to be their will (s 11A)
GIVETH AN `Is the will valid as to form`
`is the will valid as to form` `in writing` `the signer` `the place` `witnesses present` `witnesses who signed` `class` `international will` `court-ordered will` `statutory will elsewhere` `other law` `the will-maker has died` `the date of death` `the court's view` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 9 -- was the will signed and witnessed as required` `in writing` `the signer` `the place` `witnesses present` `witnesses who signed`
            THEN `valid: signed and witnessed as s 9 requires`
        IF (NOT (`class` EQUALS `none of these`))
            THEN `valid: a privileged will of a person on active service or in a like class (s 16)`
        IF `international will`
            THEN `valid: an international will under the convention (s 16K, schedule 1)`
        IF `court-ordered will`
            THEN `valid: a will made under a Supreme Court order, in writing, signed by the registrar and sealed (s 16F)`
        IF `statutory will elsewhere`
            THEN `valid: a statutory will made under the law of the place where the person lived (s 16I)`
        IF `Part 2A applies`
           AND `other law` EQUALS `the law of the place where the land it deals with is (for that land only)`
            THEN `valid so far as it deals with land in the place whose law it followed (s 15D (1) (b))`
        IF `Part 2A applies`
           AND (NOT (`other law` EQUALS `only the formal rules of the ACT Wills Act`))
            THEN `valid: properly made under the law of a place connected with the will-maker (Part 2A, s 15C, s 15D)`
        IF (NOT `the will-maker has died`)
            THEN `not valid as made, and s 11A is only for a document of a person who has died`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is satisfied`
            THEN `a will: the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended the document to be their will (s 11A)`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is not satisfied`
            THEN `not a will: the Supreme Court is not satisfied the deceased intended it to be their will (s 11A)`
        OTHERWISE `not valid as made; it is a will only if the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended it to be (s 11A)`
    WHERE
        `Part 2A applies` MEANS
            IF `the will-maker has died`
            THEN (`the calendar date` 6 10 1983) `falls before` `the date of death`
            ELSE TRUE

§§ `Alterations (s 12)`

-- s 12 (1): an alteration "made in a will after the execution of the will is not valid or effectual for any purpose,
-- except so far as a word in the will or the effect of the will before the alteration is not apparent, unless" it is
-- signed and witnessed like a will. (2): it is "deemed to comply" if the signatures are in the margin or near the
-- alteration, or at a memorandum referring to it. s 11A: a deceased's informal amendment counts if the court is satisfied.
DECLARE `Where the alteration was signed` IS ONE OF
    `in the margin, or elsewhere on the will, opposite or near the alteration`
    `at the foot or end of, or opposite, a memorandum in the will that refers to the alteration`
    `somewhere else on the will`
    `the alteration was not signed`

DECLARE `Does the alteration count` IS ONE OF
    `s 12 does not apply: the alteration was made before the will was executed`
    `the alteration counts: it was signed and witnessed as s 12 requires`
    `the alteration counts: the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended it (s 11A)`
    `whether it counts is not settled by s 12: the signatures are not where s 12 (2) deems them to comply`
    `the alteration does not count, but the will is read without the original words, which cannot be made out (s 12 (1))`
    `the alteration does not count unless the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended it (s 11A); the will is read as before`
    `the alteration does not count: the will is read as it was before`

@export Does a change made to a will count (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 12)? A crossing-out, insertion or other change made after the will was signed counts only if the change is itself signed by the will-maker (or for them) before 2 witnesses present together, who sign too; signatures in the margin, near the change or at a note referring to it are enough. Where an unwitnessed change makes the original words impossible to read, the will is read without them. For a will-maker who has died, the Supreme Court can accept a change it is satisfied they intended (s 11A).
GIVEN `made after execution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the change was made after the will was signed and witnessed
      `the signer` IS A `Who signed` @desc Who signed the change
      `where signed` IS A `Where the alteration was signed` @desc Where the signatures for the change were put
      `witnesses present` IS A NUMBER @desc How many witnesses were present together when the change was signed, or the signature acknowledged
      `witnesses who signed` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of those witnesses signed for the change in the will-maker's presence and each other's
      `original words apparent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the words (or effect) of the will before the change can still be made out
      `the will-maker has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker has died
      `the court's view` IS A `Supreme Court's view` @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended the change to amend the will (s 11A)
GIVETH A `Does the alteration count`
`s 12 -- does the change to the will count` `made after execution` `the signer` `where signed` `witnesses present` `witnesses who signed` `original words apparent` `the will-maker has died` `the court's view` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `made after execution`)
            THEN `s 12 does not apply: the alteration was made before the will was executed`
        IF `executed`
           AND `where signed` EQUALS `somewhere else on the will`
            THEN `whether it counts is not settled by s 12: the signatures are not where s 12 (2) deems them to comply`
        IF `executed`
            THEN `the alteration counts: it was signed and witnessed as s 12 requires`
        IF `the will-maker has died` AND `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is satisfied`
            THEN `the alteration counts: the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended it (s 11A)`
        IF (NOT `original words apparent`)
            THEN `the alteration does not count, but the will is read without the original words, which cannot be made out (s 12 (1))`
        IF `the will-maker has died` AND `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court has not yet decided`
            THEN `the alteration does not count unless the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended it (s 11A); the will is read as before`
        OTHERWISE `the alteration does not count: the will is read as it was before`
    WHERE
        `executed` MEANS
                `the signer` `signs for the will-maker`
            AND (NOT (`where signed` EQUALS `the alteration was not signed`))
            AND `witnesses present` AT LEAST 2
            AND `witnesses who signed` AT LEAST 2
            AND `witnesses who signed` AT MOST `witnesses present`

§§ `Rectification (s 12A)`

-- s 12A (3): "Except with the leave of the Supreme Court, an application ... for an order for rectification shall not be
-- made after the expiration of the period of 6 months commencing" on the day of (a) the public trustee and guardian's
-- notice under the Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 87B (4) or 87C (5), (b) an order under that Act s 88 (1) or
-- (3), or (c) the grant of probate or of letters of administration with the will annexed. Fork F1 counts that day in.
@export What is the last day to apply to the Supreme Court to rectify (correct) a will without needing the court's leave (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 12A (3))? The period is 6 months beginning on the day probate (or letters of administration with the will) was granted, or the day the public trustee and guardian gave notice of administering the estate, or the day of an order to collect and administer it.
GIVEN `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration with the will annexed were granted; or the day the public trustee and guardian gave notice under the Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 87B (4) or 87C (5); or the day an order under that Act s 88 (1) or (3) was granted
GIVETH A DATE
`s 12A -- the last day to apply for rectification without leave` `time began on` MEANS
    `the last day of` 6 `months commencing on` `time began on`

-- The applicant's duty with a deadline: apply within the days from the day time began to the last day. FULFILLED means
-- in time; anything else means leave is needed.
DECLARE `Applicant` IS ONE OF `the applicant`
DECLARE `Application step` IS ONE OF `apply for rectification`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant` `Application step`
`apply for rectification within 6 months` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `apply for rectification`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "no application within 6 months: an application for rectification needs the Supreme Court's leave (s 12A (3))"

-- Runs the rule over one application on a day. An application made before the day time began counts as made on day 0
-- (fork F6).
GIVEN `applied on` IS A DATE, `time began on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an application on` `applied on` `is in time, counting from` `time began on` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`apply for rectification within 6 months` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day taken`), (EVENT `the applicant` `apply for rectification` `the day taken`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` (`s 12A -- the last day to apply for rectification without leave` `time began on`)
        `the raw day` MEANS `the days from` `time began on` `to` `applied on`
        `the day taken` MEANS
            IF `the raw day` LESS THAN 0
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the raw day`

DECLARE `The court's leave` IS ONE OF
    `leave not sought or not yet decided`
    `the Supreme Court has given leave`
    `the Supreme Court has refused leave`

DECLARE `Can I apply for rectification` IS ONE OF
    `in time: you applied on or before the last day`
    `still in time: apply on or before the last day`
    `out of time: you may apply only with the Supreme Court's leave (s 12A (3))`
    `out of time, but the Supreme Court has given leave: you may apply`
    `out of time, and the Supreme Court has refused leave`

@export Am I in time to apply to the Supreme Court to rectify a will (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 12A)? The court can correct a will that fails to carry out the will-maker's intentions, or to give effect to their probable intention after circumstances they did not know of or foresee. Apply within 6 months beginning on the grant of probate (or the other starting day); later, only with the court's leave.
GIVEN `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration with the will annexed were granted (or the public trustee and guardian's notice, or the s 88 order)
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied to the Supreme Court for rectification
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day you applied (any date otherwise)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `leave` IS A `The court's leave` @desc Whether the Supreme Court has given or refused leave to apply out of time
GIVETH A `Can I apply for rectification`
`s 12A -- can I apply for rectification` `time began on` `applied` `applied on` `today` `leave` MEANS
    IF `applied` AND `an application on` `applied on` `is in time, counting from` `time began on`
    THEN `in time: you applied on or before the last day`
    ELSE IF (NOT `applied`) AND `an application on` `today` `is in time, counting from` `time began on`
         THEN `still in time: apply on or before the last day`
         ELSE CONSIDER `leave`
              WHEN `the Supreme Court has given leave` THEN `out of time, but the Supreme Court has given leave: you may apply`
              WHEN `the Supreme Court has refused leave` THEN `out of time, and the Supreme Court has refused leave`
              OTHERWISE `out of time: you may apply only with the Supreme Court's leave (s 12A (3))`

-- s 12A (4): "A personal representative ... may, within the period of 4 months commencing on the day referred to in
-- subsection (3) ..., by public notice of his or her intention to distribute ... after the expiration of the period of 1
-- month commencing on the day when the advertisement was so published and requiring any person wishing to make an
-- application for an order for rectification to do so within that period of 1 month." (The sentence has no main verb;
-- finding 1.) A notice published outside the 4 months is not a s 12A (4) notice: NOTHING (fork F7).
@export After the executor or administrator publishes a notice of intention to distribute the estate, by when does the notice ask me to apply for rectification (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 12A (4))? Within 1 month beginning on the day the notice was published, if it was published within 4 months beginning on the day time began (probate or the other starting day). The notice does not shorten the 6 months you have to apply (s 12A (3)), but once the month has passed the estate may be distributed and the personal representative is protected (s 12A (5)). No answer means the notice is not one under s 12A (4).
GIVEN `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration with the will annexed were granted (or the public trustee and guardian's notice, or the s 88 order)
      `published on` IS A DATE @desc The day the personal representative's public notice was published (on an ACT government website or in a daily newspaper circulating in the ACT)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 12A (4) -- the day the notice asks me to apply by` `time began on` `published on` MEANS
    IF      `time began on` `falls on or before` `published on`
        AND `published on` `falls on or before` (`the last day of` 4 `months commencing on` `time began on`)
    THEN JUST (`the last day of` 1 `months commencing on` `published on`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 12A (5): the personal representative "is not liable for having distributed" under the will before rectification if,
-- at the time of the distribution, (a) "a period of 1 month had elapsed since an advertisement was published in
-- accordance with subsection (4)" and they had no notice of an application; or (b) the 6 months had expired and (i) they
-- had no notice of an application, of leave, or of an application for leave, or (ii) leave was granted but "a period of 7
-- days had elapsed since the day when that leave was granted without any application". (6): nothing affects the right
-- to recover distributed property. A "not liable if": outside it, s 12A (5) gives no protection, and liability is for
-- the general law.
DECLARE `Is the personal representative protected` IS ONE OF
    `protected (s 12A (5) (a)): a month had passed since the notice and no application was known of; you may still recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
    `protected (s 12A (5) (b) (i)): the 6 months had expired and no application or leave was known of; you may still recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
    `protected (s 12A (5) (b) (ii)): 7 days had passed since leave was given without an application; you may still recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
    `s 12A (5) gives the personal representative no protection on these facts; whether they are liable is for the general law, and you may also recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`

@export If the estate was distributed under the will before the Supreme Court rectified it, is the executor or administrator protected from my claim (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 12A (5), (6))? They are protected if, when they distributed, a month had passed since their s 12A (4) notice and they knew of no application; or the 6 months had expired and they knew of no application, no leave and no application for leave; or 7 days had passed since leave was given with no application made. Either way, you can still recover the property from those who received it.
GIVEN `time began on` IS A DATE @desc The day probate or letters of administration with the will annexed were granted (or the public trustee and guardian's notice, or the s 88 order)
      `notice published` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the personal representative published a public notice of intention to distribute
      `published on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day it was published (any date otherwise)
      `distributed on` IS A DATE @desc The day the personal representative distributed the property in question
      `knew of an application` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, by that day, the personal representative had received notice that an application for rectification had been made
      `knew of leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, by that day, the personal representative had received notice that leave to apply had been sought or given
      `leave given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court had given leave to apply for rectification before that day
      `leave given on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day leave was given (any date otherwise)
      `applied after leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an application for rectification was made after leave was given and before the distribution
GIVETH AN `Is the personal representative protected`
`s 12A (5) -- is the personal representative protected` `time began on` `notice published` `published on` `distributed on` `knew of an application` `knew of leave` `leave given` `leave given on` `applied after leave` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF      `notice published`
            AND `the notice was within the 4 months`
            AND `a month had elapsed since the notice`
            AND (NOT `knew of an application`)
            THEN `protected (s 12A (5) (a)): a month had passed since the notice and no application was known of; you may still recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
        IF      `six months expired`
            AND (NOT `knew of an application`)
            AND (NOT `knew of leave`)
            THEN `protected (s 12A (5) (b) (i)): the 6 months had expired and no application or leave was known of; you may still recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
        IF      `six months expired`
            AND `leave given`
            AND (NOT `applied after leave`)
            AND `7 days had elapsed since leave`
            THEN `protected (s 12A (5) (b) (ii)): 7 days had passed since leave was given without an application; you may still recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
        OTHERWISE `s 12A (5) gives the personal representative no protection on these facts; whether they are liable is for the general law, and you may also recover the property from those who received it (s 12A (6))`
    WHERE
        `six months expired` MEANS
            (`s 12A -- the last day to apply for rectification without leave` `time began on`) `falls before` `distributed on`
        `the notice was within the 4 months` MEANS
            NOT (`s 12A (4) -- the day the notice asks me to apply by` `time began on` `published on` EQUALS NOTHING)
        `a month had elapsed since the notice` MEANS
            (`the first day after` 1 `months have elapsed since` `published on`) `falls on or before` `distributed on`
        `7 days had elapsed since leave` MEANS
            (`the date` 8 `days after` `leave given on`) `falls on or before` `distributed on`

§§ `Evidence of what the will-maker meant (s 12B)`

-- s 12B: "In proceedings to construe a will, evidence, including evidence of the testator's dispositive intention, is
-- admissible to the extent that the language used in the will renders the will ... (a) meaningless; or (b) ambiguous or
-- uncertain on the face of the will; or (c) ambiguous or uncertain in the light of the surrounding circumstances; but
-- evidence of a testator's dispositive intention is not admissible to establish any of the circumstances referred to in
-- paragraph (c)." A grant limited in its own words: outside it, NOTHING (the general law decides); the flat "not
-- admissible": JUST FALSE.
DECLARE `How the will reads` IS ONE OF
    `meaningless`
    `ambiguous or uncertain on its face`
    `ambiguous or uncertain in the light of the surrounding circumstances`
    `clear`

DECLARE `What the evidence is for` IS ONE OF
    `to work out what the will means`
    `to show the surrounding circumstances that make the will ambiguous or uncertain`

@export Can evidence of what the will-maker meant be used in court to work out what their will means (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 12B)? Yes, to the extent the will's language makes it meaningless, or ambiguous or uncertain on its face or in the light of the surrounding circumstances. But evidence of what the will-maker intended to give cannot be used to show those surrounding circumstances. No answer means s 12B does not decide it (the general law does).
GIVEN `how it reads` IS A `How the will reads` @desc How the language of the will (or the part in question) reads
      `what for` IS A `What the evidence is for` @desc What the evidence is offered to show
      `dispositive intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence is of the will-maker's intention about who should get what (for example their statements)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 12B -- can the evidence be used` `how it reads` `what for` `dispositive intention` MEANS
    IF `what for` EQUALS `to show the surrounding circumstances that make the will ambiguous or uncertain`
    THEN IF `dispositive intention`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE NOTHING
    ELSE IF `how it reads` EQUALS `clear`
         THEN NOTHING
         ELSE JUST TRUE

§§ `A will for a person without testamentary capacity (Part 3A)`

-- s 16A (1): the court may authorise a will to be made, altered or revoked "for a person who does not have testamentary
-- capacity"; (3) it "must not make an order ... unless the person ... is alive when the order is made"; s 16B (1) only
-- with leave; s 16E: the court "must refuse leave ... unless the court is satisfied" of (a)-(e); s 16C (1): on the leave
-- hearing it may give leave and make the order. s 16F: the will must be in writing, signed by the registrar and sealed.
DECLARE `Can the court make a will for them` IS ONE OF
    `no: the person has died, and the court must not make the order (s 16A (3))`
    `not under Part 3A: it is for a person who does not have testamentary capacity`
    `apply first for the Supreme Court's leave, with the s 16B information; leave must be refused unless the court is satisfied of the s 16E matters`
    `leave must be refused: the Supreme Court is not satisfied of the s 16E matters`
    `the Supreme Court may give leave and make the order (s 16C); the will must be in writing, signed by the registrar and sealed while the person is alive (s 16F)`

@export Can I ask the Supreme Court to make, change or revoke a will for someone who does not have testamentary capacity, such as a parent with dementia (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) Part 3A)? Only while the person is alive, and only with the court's leave. The court must refuse leave unless satisfied there is reason to believe the person cannot make a will, the proposed will is one they would likely have made, the order may be appropriate, you are an appropriate applicant, and everyone with a legitimate interest can be represented.
GIVEN `the person is alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person for whom the will is sought is alive
      `lacks capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person does not have testamentary capacity (cannot make a valid will themselves)
      `the court's view` IS A `Supreme Court's view` @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied of the s 16E matters: reason to believe the person is or is likely to be incapable of making a will; the proposed will is or is likely to be one they would have made; the order is or may be appropriate; the applicant is an appropriate person; and those with a legitimate interest can be represented
GIVETH A `Can the court make a will for them`
`Part 3A -- can the court make a will for someone without capacity` `the person is alive` `lacks capacity` `the court's view` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the person is alive`)
            THEN `no: the person has died, and the court must not make the order (s 16A (3))`
        IF (NOT `lacks capacity`)
            THEN `not under Part 3A: it is for a person who does not have testamentary capacity`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is satisfied`
            THEN `the Supreme Court may give leave and make the order (s 16C); the will must be in writing, signed by the registrar and sealed while the person is alive (s 16F)`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is not satisfied`
            THEN `leave must be refused: the Supreme Court is not satisfied of the s 16E matters`
        OTHERWISE `apply first for the Supreme Court's leave, with the s 16B information; leave must be refused unless the court is satisfied of the s 16E matters`

§§ `Marriage after the will (s 20)`

-- s 20 (1): "if a person marries or enters into a civil union or civil partnership after having made a will, the will is
-- revoked by the marriage ... unless the will was expressed to have been made in contemplation of that marriage"; (2)
-- an exercise of a power of appointment survives where the property would not, in default, pass to the executor or
-- administrator; (3) a gift to the person, expressed to be in contemplation of marrying them, is not revoked, and the
-- rest is not revoked "unless a contrary intention appears". s 22 (3): a revived will is taken to be made when revived.
DECLARE `What the marriage did to the will` IS ONE OF
    `s 20 does not arise: no marriage, civil union or civil partnership after the will was made`
    `not revoked: the will was expressed to be made in contemplation of that marriage, civil union or civil partnership (s 20 (1))`
    `not revoked: the gift to the person married stands, and so does the rest of the will (s 20 (3))`
    `the gift to the person married stands, but the rest of the will is revoked: a contrary intention appears (s 20 (3) (b))`
    `revoked, except so far as it exercises a power of appointment over property that would not pass to an executor or administrator in default (s 20 (2))`
    `revoked by the marriage, civil union or civil partnership (s 20 (1))`

@export Did the will-maker's marriage (or civil union or civil partnership) after making the will revoke it (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 20)? It does, unless the will says it was made in contemplation of that marriage. A gift in the will to the new spouse, said to be in contemplation of marrying them, survives, and so does the rest of the will unless a contrary intention appears.
GIVEN `married after` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker married, or entered a civil union or civil partnership, after making the will (a revived will counts as made when revived)
      `whole will in contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will says it was made in contemplation of that marriage, civil union or civil partnership
      `gift in contemplation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will gives property (or a power of appointment) to that person, said to be in contemplation of marrying them or entering the civil union or partnership with them
      `contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will (or evidence admitted under s 12B) shows an intention that the rest of the will should be revoked by the marriage
      `power of appointment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will exercises a power of appointment over property that, if the power were not exercised, would not pass to an executor or administrator of the will-maker
GIVETH A `What the marriage did to the will`
`s 20 -- did the marriage revoke the will` `married after` `whole will in contemplation` `gift in contemplation` `contrary intention` `power of appointment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `married after`)
            THEN `s 20 does not arise: no marriage, civil union or civil partnership after the will was made`
        IF `whole will in contemplation`
            THEN `not revoked: the will was expressed to be made in contemplation of that marriage, civil union or civil partnership (s 20 (1))`
        IF `gift in contemplation` AND (NOT `contrary intention`)
            THEN `not revoked: the gift to the person married stands, and so does the rest of the will (s 20 (3))`
        IF `gift in contemplation`
            THEN `the gift to the person married stands, but the rest of the will is revoked: a contrary intention appears (s 20 (3) (b))`
        IF `power of appointment`
            THEN `revoked, except so far as it exercises a power of appointment over property that would not pass to an executor or administrator in default (s 20 (2))`
        OTHERWISE `revoked by the marriage, civil union or civil partnership (s 20 (1))`

§§ `Divorce after the will (s 20A)`

-- s 20A (1): if, after the will is made, the marriage, civil union or civil partnership "is terminated", (a) a beneficial
-- gift to the former partner (not a charge or direction to pay a debt) and a power of appointment given to them are
-- revoked, (b) an appointment of them as executor, trustee or guardian is "taken to be omitted", (c) the property passes
-- "as if the former spouse ... had predeceased the testator". (2): not if the court is satisfied the will-maker did not
-- intend to revoke, or the will was republished after the termination without showing that intention. (4): what counts
-- as termination. (3): the former partner's right to apply under the Family Provision Act 1969 is not affected.
DECLARE `How the relationship ended` IS ONE OF
    `divorce under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth)`
    `a decree of nullity under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth)`
    `an annulment outside Australia recognised in Australia under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cwlth)`
    `a civil union ended under the Civil Unions Act 2012, division 2.4, other than by death`
    `a civil partnership ended under the Domestic Relationships Act 1994, division 4A.4, other than by death`
    `separation only, or it has not ended in any of those ways`

DECLARE `What the will gives the former partner` IS ONE OF
    `a gift, or a power of appointment`
    `an appointment as executor, trustee or guardian`
    `a charge or direction to pay a debt owed to them`

DECLARE `What the divorce did to the will` IS ONE OF
    `s 20A does not apply: the relationship has not been terminated in a way s 20A (4) describes`
    `s 20A does not apply: the relationship ended before the will was made`
    `not affected: a provision to pay a debt owed to the former partner stands (s 20A (1) (a), (3) (b))`
    `not revoked: the will was republished after the termination without showing an intention to revoke (s 20A (2) (b))`
    `not revoked: the Supreme Court is satisfied the will-maker did not intend to revoke it (s 20A (2) (a))`
    `revoked unless the Supreme Court is satisfied the will-maker did not intend to revoke it (s 20A (1), (2) (a))`
    `revoked: the property passes as if the former partner had died before the will-maker (s 20A (1) (a), (c))`
    `omitted: the will is read as if the former partner had not been appointed (s 20A (1) (b))`

@export What does a divorce (or the end of a civil union or civil partnership) do to a gift or appointment in the will in favour of the former partner (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 20A)? If the relationship ended after the will was made, a gift to the former partner is revoked and passes as if they had died first, and their appointment as executor, trustee or guardian is dropped -- unless the will was republished afterwards or the Supreme Court is satisfied the will-maker did not intend that. A provision to pay a debt owed to them stands. Separation alone does not count. The former partner can still apply for family provision.
GIVEN `how it ended` IS A `How the relationship ended` @desc How the will-maker's marriage, civil union or civil partnership with the former partner ended
      `ended after the will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it ended after the will was made
      `what the will gives` IS A `What the will gives the former partner` @desc The provision of the will in question
      `republished` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after it ended, the will was republished by a later will or codicil that shows no intention to revoke the provision
      `the court's view` IS A `Supreme Court's view` @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied that the will-maker, when the relationship ended, did not intend to revoke the provision
GIVETH A `What the divorce did to the will`
`s 20A -- what the divorce did to the provision for the former partner` `how it ended` `ended after the will` `what the will gives` `republished` `the court's view` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `how it ended` EQUALS `separation only, or it has not ended in any of those ways`
            THEN `s 20A does not apply: the relationship has not been terminated in a way s 20A (4) describes`
        IF (NOT `ended after the will`)
            THEN `s 20A does not apply: the relationship ended before the will was made`
        IF `what the will gives` EQUALS `a charge or direction to pay a debt owed to them`
            THEN `not affected: a provision to pay a debt owed to the former partner stands (s 20A (1) (a), (3) (b))`
        IF `republished`
            THEN `not revoked: the will was republished after the termination without showing an intention to revoke (s 20A (2) (b))`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court is satisfied`
            THEN `not revoked: the Supreme Court is satisfied the will-maker did not intend to revoke it (s 20A (2) (a))`
        IF `the court's view` EQUALS `the Supreme Court has not yet decided`
            THEN `revoked unless the Supreme Court is satisfied the will-maker did not intend to revoke it (s 20A (1), (2) (a))`
        IF `what the will gives` EQUALS `an appointment as executor, trustee or guardian`
            THEN `omitted: the will is read as if the former partner had not been appointed (s 20A (1) (b))`
        OTHERWISE `revoked: the property passes as if the former partner had died before the will-maker (s 20A (1) (a), (c))`

§§ `Revocation and revival (s 21, s 22)`

-- s 21: "Subject to sections 8B, 16A, 20 and 20A, a will or part of a will is not revoked except -- (a) [a s 16 person] by
-- ... expressing his or her intention to revoke ...; and (b) ... (i) by a subsequent valid will ...; or (ii) by the
-- testator executing a document in like manner as a will ... that shows his or her intention to revoke ...; or (iii) by
-- the burning, tearing or otherwise destroying of the will ... by the testator, or by a person acting in the presence of
-- and by the direction of the testator, with the intention of revoking". s 11A: a deceased's informal revocation, if
-- the court is satisfied. A closed list: none of them, the text's no.
DECLARE `What happened to the will` IS ONE OF
    `it was not destroyed`
    `the will-maker destroyed it, meaning to revoke it`
    `someone else destroyed it in the will-maker's presence and at their direction, the will-maker meaning to revoke it`
    `someone else destroyed it, but not in the will-maker's presence or not at their direction`
    `it was destroyed without any intention of revoking it`

DECLARE `Has the will been revoked` IS ONE OF
    `revoked by a later valid will (s 21 (b) (i))`
    `revoked by a document executed like a will showing the intention to revoke (s 21 (b) (ii))`
    `revoked by being destroyed with the intention of revoking it (s 21 (b) (iii))`
    `revoked by a privileged declaration of a person on active service or in a like class (s 21 (a), s 16)`
    `revoked by a later marriage, civil union or civil partnership (s 20)`
    `revoked under a Supreme Court order (s 8B, s 16A)`
    `revoked: the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended a document to revoke it (s 11A)`
    `not revoked (s 21)`

@export Has the will been revoked (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 21)? Only by a later valid will; by a document signed and witnessed like a will that shows the intention to revoke; by the will-maker (or someone in their presence and at their direction) burning, tearing or destroying it meaning to revoke it; by a privileged declaration of a person on active service; by a later marriage (s 20); by a court order (s 8B, s 16A); or by a document the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased meant to revoke it (s 11A). A divorce revokes only the gifts to the former partner (s 20A).
GIVEN `later will` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a later valid will of the will-maker revokes this will (or the part in question)
      `revoking document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker signed a document, witnessed as a will must be, that shows the intention to revoke the will
      `destruction` IS A `What happened to the will` @desc Whether, and how, the will was destroyed
      `privileged revocation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker, while in one of the s 16 classes (Defence Force on active service and the like), expressed an intention to revoke it, orally or in writing
      `revoked by marriage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a later marriage, civil union or civil partnership revoked it under s 20 (see that question)
      `court order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court authorised its revocation under s 8B (a child's will) or s 16A (a person without testamentary capacity), and the revocation followed the order
      `informal revocation found` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court is satisfied under s 11A that the will-maker, now deceased, intended a document to revoke the will
GIVETH A `Has the will been revoked`
`s 21 -- has the will been revoked` `later will` `revoking document` `destruction` `privileged revocation` `revoked by marriage` `court order` `informal revocation found` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `later will`
            THEN `revoked by a later valid will (s 21 (b) (i))`
        IF `revoking document`
            THEN `revoked by a document executed like a will showing the intention to revoke (s 21 (b) (ii))`
        IF     `destruction` EQUALS `the will-maker destroyed it, meaning to revoke it`
            OR `destruction` EQUALS `someone else destroyed it in the will-maker's presence and at their direction, the will-maker meaning to revoke it`
            THEN `revoked by being destroyed with the intention of revoking it (s 21 (b) (iii))`
        IF `privileged revocation`
            THEN `revoked by a privileged declaration of a person on active service or in a like class (s 21 (a), s 16)`
        IF `revoked by marriage`
            THEN `revoked by a later marriage, civil union or civil partnership (s 20)`
        IF `court order`
            THEN `revoked under a Supreme Court order (s 8B, s 16A)`
        IF `informal revocation found`
            THEN `revoked: the Supreme Court is satisfied the deceased intended a document to revoke it (s 11A)`
        OTHERWISE `not revoked (s 21)`

-- s 22 (1): "A will ... that has been revoked is not revived unless -- (a) the testator re-executes it in the manner in
-- which a valid will is required by part 2 to be executed; or (b) the testator executes, in [that] manner ..., a valid
-- codicil that shows the intention of the testator to revive the will." (3): a revived will is taken to be made when
-- revived. A closed condition: the text's no otherwise.
@export Has a revoked will been brought back into force (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 22)? Only if the will-maker signs it again before witnesses as a will must be signed, or signs and has witnessed a codicil that shows the intention to revive it. A revived will counts as made on the day it is revived (so a marriage before that day does not revoke it).
GIVEN `re-executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker signed the revoked will again, witnessed as a will must be (s 9)
      `reviving codicil` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will-maker signed, with witnesses as a will must be, a valid codicil that shows the intention to revive the will
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 22 -- has the revoked will been revived` `re-executed` `reviving codicil` IF
    `re-executed` OR `reviving codicil`

§§ `Surviving the will-maker by 30 days (s 31C)`

-- s 31C (1): it applies if a person is given property by will, or takes on intestacy, and "(b) the person does not
-- survive the deceased person by 30 days"; (2) the person "is taken to have predeceased the deceased person and the
-- devise ... lapses, unless the contrary intention appears"; (3) not where the estate would then pass to the Territory
-- (Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 49CA); (4) a general survivorship condition is not a contrary intention.
-- Fork F3 for the count.
DECLARE `Does the gift lapse` IS ONE OF
    `does not lapse under s 31C: the beneficiary survived the deceased by 30 days`
    `not yet known: the 30 days after the death have not ended`
    `lapses: the beneficiary is taken to have died before the deceased (s 31C (2))`
    `does not lapse: a contrary intention appears from the will or from evidence under s 12B (s 31C (2))`
    `does not lapse: otherwise the estate would pass to the Territory (s 31C (3))`

@export Does a gift in a will (or a share on intestacy) lapse because the beneficiary did not survive the deceased by 30 days (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 31C)? A beneficiary who does not outlive the deceased by 30 days is treated as having died first, and the gift lapses, unless the will (or admissible evidence) shows otherwise or the estate would otherwise go to the Territory. A will's general requirement to survive does not count as showing otherwise.
GIVEN `the deceased died on` IS A DATE @desc The day the deceased (the will-maker, or the person whose estate is shared on intestacy) died
      `the beneficiary has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary has died
      `the beneficiary died on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the day the beneficiary died (any date otherwise)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will, or evidence admitted under s 12B, shows an intention that the gift should not lapse (a general requirement to survive the will-maker does not count)
      `estate to the Territory` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, if the gift lapsed, the deceased's estate would pass to the Territory under the Administration and Probate Act 1929 s 49CA
GIVETH A `Does the gift lapse`
`s 31C -- does the gift lapse for not surviving by 30 days` `the deceased died on` `the beneficiary has died` `the beneficiary died on` `today` `contrary intention` `estate to the Territory` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the beneficiary has died` AND `a person who died on` `the beneficiary died on` `survived the death on` `the deceased died on` `by 30 days`
            THEN `does not lapse under s 31C: the beneficiary survived the deceased by 30 days`
        IF (NOT `the beneficiary has died`) AND `a person who died on` `today` `survived the death on` `the deceased died on` `by 30 days`
            THEN `does not lapse under s 31C: the beneficiary survived the deceased by 30 days`
        IF (NOT `the beneficiary has died`)
            THEN `not yet known: the 30 days after the death have not ended`
        IF `contrary intention`
            THEN `does not lapse: a contrary intention appears from the will or from evidence under s 12B (s 31C (2))`
        IF `estate to the Territory`
            THEN `does not lapse: otherwise the estate would pass to the Territory (s 31C (3))`
        OTHERWISE `lapses: the beneficiary is taken to have died before the deceased (s 31C (2))`

§§ `Gifts to children and other issue (s 31, s 31B)`

-- The share rule common to s 31 (2) and s 31B (1)-(3): the property is divided into as many equal shares as there are
-- nearest issue who survive the will-maker by 30 days, plus nearest issue who did not but left issue who did; a
-- surviving nearest issue takes 1 share; the surviving issue of a nearest issue who did not survive take that 1 share
-- equally. The nearest issue are taken to be children (of the original beneficiary under s 31, of the will-maker under
-- s 31B); remoter issue taking a deceased grandchild's share (s 31 (3), s 31B (3)) are out (NOTHING; fork F8).
DECLARE `My place among the issue` IS ONE OF
    `a child who survived the will-maker by 30 days`
    `a grandchild who survived the will-maker by 30 days, whose parent did not`
    `a more remote descendant`
    `I did not survive the will-maker by 30 days`

GIVEN `my place` IS A `My place among the issue`, `children surviving` IS A NUMBER, `children who left issue` IS A NUMBER, `my branch` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the issue share for` `my place` `children surviving` `children who left issue` `my branch` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my place`
    WHEN `a child who survived the will-maker by 30 days` THEN
        IF `the shares` AT LEAST 1
        THEN JUST (1 DIVIDED BY `the shares`)
        ELSE JUST 0
    WHEN `a grandchild who survived the will-maker by 30 days, whose parent did not` THEN
        IF `the shares` AT LEAST 1 AND `my branch` AT LEAST 1
        THEN JUST (1 DIVIDED BY (`the shares` TIMES `my branch`))
        ELSE JUST 0
    WHEN `a more remote descendant` THEN NOTHING
    WHEN `I did not survive the will-maker by 30 days` THEN JUST 0
    WHERE
        `the shares` MEANS `children surviving` PLUS `children who left issue`

-- s 31 (1): "If -- (a) a testator ... devises or bequeaths property to ... a person (the original beneficiary) ... who is
-- a child or other issue of the testator for an estate or interest not determinable before or on the death of the
-- original beneficiary; and (b) the original beneficiary dies in the lifetime of the testator and is survived by issue;
-- and (c) any such issue survive the testator for a period of 30 days ...; then, unless a contrary intention appears
-- ..., the will has force and effect as if the devise ... were to ... any issue of the original beneficiary who survive
-- the testator for the specified period". (4) a general survival or age condition is not a contrary intention; (5) not
-- if the original beneficiary had not met another contingency. An "if" list: when it fails, s 31 gives nothing (0).
@export My parent was left a gift in the will of their parent or grandparent, but died before the will-maker. What share of that gift do I take (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 31)? Unless the will shows otherwise, the gift passes to the original beneficiary's children who survive the will-maker by 30 days, in equal shares; the children of a child who did not survive take that child's share equally. The answer is the fraction of the gift you take (0 if s 31 gives you nothing; no answer for more remote descendants).
GIVEN `issue of the will-maker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the original beneficiary (your parent) was a child or other descendant of the will-maker
      `interest for life only` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift to the original beneficiary was one that would end on or before their death (a life interest, for example)
      `died before the will-maker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the original beneficiary died before the will-maker
      `other contingency unmet` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will made the gift depend on some other condition (not surviving the will-maker or reaching an age) that the original beneficiary had not met
      `contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will, or evidence admitted under s 12B, shows that the gift should not pass to the original beneficiary's descendants (a general requirement to survive or to reach an age does not count)
      `my place` IS A `My place among the issue` @desc Your place among the original beneficiary's descendants
      `children surviving` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the original beneficiary's children survived the will-maker by 30 days
      `children who left issue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the original beneficiary's children did not survive the will-maker by 30 days but left children of their own who did
      `my branch` IS A NUMBER @desc If you are a grandchild: how many children of your parent (including you) survived the will-maker by 30 days; otherwise 0
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 31 -- my share of my parent's gift` `issue of the will-maker` `interest for life only` `died before the will-maker` `other contingency unmet` `contrary intention` `my place` `children surviving` `children who left issue` `my branch` MEANS
    IF      `issue of the will-maker`
        AND (NOT `interest for life only`)
        AND `died before the will-maker`
        AND (NOT `other contingency unmet`)
        AND (NOT `contrary intention`)
        AND `children surviving` PLUS `children who left issue` AT LEAST 1
    THEN `the issue share for` `my place` `children surviving` `children who left issue` `my branch`
    ELSE JUST 0

-- s 31B (1): "If a testator by will devises or bequeaths property to ... his or her issue then, unless a contrary
-- intention appears ..., the testator is presumed to have intended that ... [it] is to be distributed in equal shares
-- between only those issue of the testator who -- (a) are his or her nearest issue; and (b) survive the testator for a
-- period of 30 days"; (2) the surviving issue of a nearest issue who dies within the period take that share equally;
-- (3) remoter issue only through a parent who died. A presumption: the answer is the presumed share.
@export The will leaves property to the will-maker's "issue" (descendants) as a group. What share do I take (Wills Act 1968 (ACT) s 31B)? Unless the will shows otherwise, it is shared equally among the will-maker's children who survive them by 30 days; the children of a child who did not survive that long share that child's part equally. The answer is the fraction you take (no answer for more remote descendants, or where a contrary intention means s 31B does not decide it).
GIVEN `contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will, or evidence admitted under s 12B, shows a different intention for how the gift to the issue is shared
      `my place` IS A `My place among the issue` @desc Your place among the will-maker's descendants
      `children surviving` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the will-maker's children survived the will-maker by 30 days
      `children who left issue` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the will-maker's children did not survive the will-maker by 30 days (including those who died first) but left children who did
      `my branch` IS A NUMBER @desc If you are a grandchild: how many children of your parent (including you) survived the will-maker by 30 days; otherwise 0
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 31B -- my share of a gift to the will-maker's issue` `contrary intention` `my place` `children surviving` `children who left issue` `my branch` MEANS
    IF `contrary intention`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE `the issue share for` `my place` `children surviving` `children who left issue` `my branch`
